# Chapter 7, part 1: what the Document Manager is, how it starts, the screen
# and the keys.
.h2 What the Document Manager is [str-what]
.ix Document Manager, purpose
.ix Master menu
.ix Host program
The Document Manager is the menu that starts a session.  The work on a document
is left to the Editor: the entry Edit / Preview / Print Document opens the
document in the Editor.  When the Editor ends, the Document Manager is on the
screen again, with the same entry highlighted.
.h3 The Document Manager and the Editor's menu [str-two]
.ix Document Manager, inside the editor
.ix Two Document Managers
Strata has two screens of this kind.
.table 19 41
Screen | What it is
The Document Manager | The screen of this chapter: a menu, panels with
 | fields, a Setup panel, a list of fonts.  It is the usual way to start
 | Strata.
The Editor's own menu | The Editor, started with no document name, shows a
 | smaller menu over an empty editing screen. A file name is requested in a
 | one line box, and opens the panels of Maintain Dictionaries and Compare
 | Document Versions.  See {x:str-inside}.
.end
The settings made in the Setup panel of the Document Manager reach the
Editor through the file {c:~/.strata/config} (see {n:appcfg}).
.h3 Spell check [str-two-print]
.ix Spell check, Document Manager
The Document Manager has no entry of its own for spell checking a document:
Spell is in the Tools menu of the Editor ({n:spell}).

.h2 Starting the Document Manager [str-start]
.ix Starting the Document Manager
.ix Command line, Document Manager
.ix Starting Strata
.ix Document Manager, options
The Document Manager is started with no argument, or with the options and
the word of the table below.  The full list of programs, with their
options, is in {n:appcmd}.
.table 26 34
Argument | Effect
{c:-H FILE}, {c:--help-file FILE} | Use FILE as the help file.
{c:-h}, {c:--usage} | Print the usage text (the options, in a few lines) on the
 | standard output and end with status 0, without opening the screen.
{c:--version} | Print the name and the version of the program on the standard
 | output, then a second line with the version of the file format that it
 | writes ({c:file format 3}), and end with status 0, without opening the
 | screen.
{c:--default-colors} | Use the colors that the program is delivered with and
 | ignore the color settings.  The variable {c:STRATA_DEFAULT_COLORS} set to 1
 | does the same ({n:str-colours}).
DOCUMENT | The name of a document, put in the field Document Name
 | of the panel Edit / Preview / Print Document.  Nothing is
 | opened.  Of several such words the last one counts.
.end
.h3 What happens at start [str-start-order]
.ix Start, order of events
.ix Search for the editor
.ix Search for the help file
At start the Document Manager takes your settings (see {n:appcfg}), looks for
the help file and for the Editor, and shows the menu with Edit / Preview /
Print Document highlighted.  A missing or unusable settings file gives the
defaults.  Without an interactive terminal (or, in the X11 version, without an
X display) it writes a message and ends.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: at start the Document Manager does this, in order.
1. It reads the settings from {c:~/.strata/config} (see {n:appcfg}).  The
 values that the Document Manager uses are {c:workdir}, the Editor program,
 {c:help}, {c:autosave}, {c:autosave_seconds}, {c:printer} and
 {c:spell_language}.
2. The command line is read.
3. The help file is looked for, when {c:-H} was given, when the setting
 {c:help} is empty, or when the file it names cannot be read (see
 {n:str-help-search}).  If none is found the program starts without help
 (see {n:str-help}).
4. The Editor program is looked for (see {n:str-find-editor}).
5.  The working directory is chosen: the setting {c:workdir} if it names a
directory that exists, otherwise the current directory.
6. It shows the menu with Edit / Preview / Print Document highlighted.  With
no interactive terminal the program writes a message on the standard error
and ends with status 1.
.endif
.h3 The help file [str-help-search]
.ix Help file, search path
.ix STRATA_HELP
The help file is the first of these that can be read.
.table 5 55
Step | Place
1 | The file named by {c:-H}.
2 | The file named by the setting Help file of Setup.
3 | The file named by the environment variable {c:STRATA_HELP}.
4 | The file {c:strata.hlp} in the current directory.
5 | The file {c:strata.hlp} next to the program, in the directory above it, or
 | in {c:share/strata} of the directory above it.
6 | The file {c:strata.hlp} in {c:/usr/local/share/strata} and then in
 | {c:/usr/share/strata}.
.end
When {c:-H} names a file that cannot be read, the search goes on with the
other places, and a file that it finds in step 3 or later takes the place of
the setting.  The setting is used as it is when {c:-H} is not given and the
file that the setting names can be read: {c:STRATA_HELP} is then not looked at.
An empty {c:STRATA_HELP} is skipped.  Without a help file the program works as
usual and {k:F1} shows a notice (see {n:str-help}).
.h3 Finding the editor [str-find-editor]
.ix Editor program
.ix Editor program, environment variable
.ix Editor, how the Document Manager finds it
The Document Manager finds the Editor that was installed with it, so you
need not name it.  The field Editor program of the Setup panel is for an
Editor that is kept somewhere else; its value is used as typed, without a
check.  A wrong value shows up as the message "Cannot run the editor: PATH"
when Edit / Preview / Print Document is chosen (see {n:str-messages}).  When
the Editor is the one that was found by itself, the Setup panel does not save
it; only a path that you chose is kept.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the Editor is started from a path that is chosen like this.
1. If the Editor program setting of the configuration file names a file that
 exists and may be run, that file is used.
2. Otherwise the environment variable that names the Editor program, if it is
 not empty (the variable is named in {n:appcmd}).
3. Otherwise the Editor program that is in the directory that holds the
 Document Manager program itself, if it may be run.
4. Otherwise the Editor program that is found in the system directories
 ({n:appcmd-find}), which is looked for each time the Editor is started.
The check is made once, at start.
.endif
.h3 Exit status of the Document Manager [str-exit]
.ix Exit status, Document Manager
.ix Document Manager, exit codes
.table 8 52
Status | Meaning
0 | Quit was chosen, or {c:-h}, {c:--usage} or {c:--version} was given.
1 | There is no interactive terminal or X display.
.end
The failure of a program that the Document Manager started is shown on the
screen and the menu stays.

.h2 The screen [str-screen]
.ix Screen, Document Manager
.ix Footer line
.ix Message line, Document Manager
.ix Status line, Document Manager
A screen of the Document Manager has three parts.
.table 12 48
Part | Content
The window | A box, centered, with a title in its top border and the
 | instruction of the panel in bold in its bottom border: "Press Ins to
 | Select Action" for the menus, "Press Ins to Use Displayed Settings"
 | for the panels.  The title of the main menu is "Strata Document
 | Manager".
The panel | The entries of a menu, one to a line, or the fields of a form,
 | each with its label at the left and its value at the right.
The footer | The last line of the screen, in reverse video.  It shows
 | "F1 Help   Esc Cancel" until a message is to be shown; then
 | that message is shown in bold.  A message stays until the next key.
.end
.pic pictures/str-main.png 5 The Document Manager.
.h3 Fields in a panel [str-fields]
.ix Field, appearance
.ix Dots in a field
.ix Dimmed field
A field of a panel is drawn as a label, right aligned against a colon, and
a value area filled with middle dots where there is no text.  The dots show
how long the field is.  The value can be much longer than the area (a name
may have up to 4095 characters); the area then scrolls so that the cursor
stays visible, and only the field that has the focus scrolls.
.table 24 36
Appearance | Meaning
Normal label, dots | A field whose value can be changed.
Value in reverse video | The field has the focus and its value is
 | untouched: the first character typed replaces the whole value (see
 | {n:str-keys-panel}).
Block cursor | The place where the next character goes.
.end

.h2 Keys [str-keys]
.ix Keys, Document Manager
.ix Keyboard, Document Manager
Three kinds of screen take keys: the menus, the panels (forms) and the
lists (selection boxes).  A fourth, the help viewer, is described in
{x:str-help}.  The keys are the same in the terminal and in the X11
version.
.h3 Menus [str-keys-menu]
.ix Menu keys, Document Manager
.ix Main menu, keys
These keys work in the Document Manager menu and in the menu TeX Font
Association.
.table 17 43
Key | Effect
{k:Up} | Highlight the previous entry; from the first, the last.
{k:Down} | Highlight the next entry; from the last, the first.
{k:Home} | Highlight the first entry.
{k:End} | Highlight the last entry.
A letter or digit | Highlight the next entry, counting on from the
 | highlighted one, whose first character is that letter (capital or
 | small).  The entry is not chosen.  Press the letter again for the next
 | entry that begins with it, and round to the first.
{k:Enter} {k:Ins} | Choose the highlighted entry.
{k:Esc} | TeX Font Association: leave the menu.  Document Manager menu:
 | nothing.
{k:F1} | Help on the menu (see {n:str-help}).
.end
Any other key is ignored.  After a panel is left, the menu is drawn again
with the entry that was chosen highlighted.
.h3 Panels [str-keys-panel]
.ix Panel keys, Document Manager
.ix Form keys
.ix Field editing keys
.ix Replace on first key
These are the keys of every form of the Document Manager: Edit / Preview /
Print Document, New
File, Setup and the panel of a font.
.table 19 41
Key | Effect
{k:Ins} | Accept the panel and act on it.  It works in any field.
{k:Esc} | Cancel the panel: nothing is changed or saved.  The program goes
 | back to the menu (from New File, back to Edit / Preview / Print Document).
{k:Enter} | Accept the field and go to the next one.  In the last field it
 | accepts the panel, as {k:Ins} does.
{k:Down} {k:Tab} | The next field; from the last, the first.
{k:Up} {k:Shift-Tab} | The previous field; from the first, the last.
{k:Left} {k:Right} | Move the cursor one character in the value.
{k:Home} {k:End} | Move the cursor to the start or to the end of the value.
{k:Backspace} | Delete the character before the cursor.
{k:Del} | Delete the character under the cursor.
A printable character | Insert it at the cursor.
{k:Keypad +} {k:Alt-+} {k:+} {k:F2} | On a field that names a file, a
 | directory or a printer: list the choices ({n:str-browse}).
{k:F1} | Help on the panel (see {n:str-help}).
.end
When the focus comes to a field that has text, the text is shown in reverse
video.  The text is then selected: the first character that is typed
replaces the whole value, and {k:Backspace} or {k:Del} empties it.  Any of
{k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Home} and {k:End} ends the selection and leaves the
value as it is, so that it can be edited in place.  An empty field has no
selection.
.caution The plus sign is taken by the listing on every field that names a
  file, a directory or a printer, so a plus sign cannot be typed in the
  fields Document Name, Printer, Working Directory, Initial Working
  Directory, Editor program and Help file.  It can be typed
  in the other fields.
.h3 Lists and notices [str-keys-list]
.ix List keys
.ix Selection list
.ix Notice, any key
.ix Press any key
Small boxes appear on top of a screen.
.table 20 40
Box | Keys
A list (the file list, the choice of document type, the list of printers)
 | {k:Up} {k:Down} {k:Home} {k:End} {k:PgUp} {k:PgDn} move the highlight;
 | {k:Enter} chooses; {k:Esc} leaves with no choice.  A letter or digit
 | moves to the next entry that begins with it and, in a list of twelve
 | entries or fewer, chooses it at once.
A notice ("Press any key") | Any key closes it.  It has a title and one
 | line of text; a longer text is wrapped in a box of up to twelve
 | lines.
.end
.note A list is moved by the letter keys in a different way from a menu: in
  a list of twelve entries or fewer a letter chooses the entry without
  {k:Enter}.  In the file list this can be a surprise when the directory has
  few files: pressing {k:m} chooses the first entry that begins with m.
